description:
crate-jmap-server/src/backend.rs:499-565 — BackendChangesError::TooManyChanges carries a u64 limit field whose value 0 is the convention for "cannot calculate changes" and any non-zero value is the suggested retry window. The pattern is documented in the type:

  TooManyChanges {
      // ...
      // A value of `0` signals a full state
      // reset is required per RFC 8620 §5.6; any non-zero value is
      // the suggested maximum the client may retry with.
      limit: u64,
  },

The Display impl pattern-matches on the literal zero (line 526-533):

  Self::TooManyChanges { limit: 0 } => write!(f, "cannot calculate changes"),
  Self::TooManyChanges { limit } => write!(f, "too many changes (limit: {limit})"),

And the From impl does likewise (line 551-565):

  BackendChangesError::TooManyChanges { limit: 0 } => jmap_types::JmapError::cannot_calculate_changes(),
  BackendChangesError::TooManyChanges { limit } => jmap_types::JmapError::too_many_changes_with_limit(limit),

A serious Rust practitioner reads this and winces. The pattern is exactly what enums exist to eliminate: distinct semantic states represented as distinct constructors, not as magic-value overloads of one constructor. The corresponding JMAP wire types ARE distinct ("cannotCalculateChanges" vs "tooManyChanges" — see JmapError methods called above), but the backend-error envelope flattens them into one variant + a sentinel.

What goes wrong if it stays as-is:

  1. A backend implementor reading the type signature `TooManyChanges { limit: u64 }` has to also read the docstring AND the Display impl to learn that limit=0 has different semantics. That is the discoverability cost of bool-ish parameters and magic-value overloads — the type carries less information than its content actually has.
  2. A backend that legitimately wants to suggest a 0-entry batch (degenerate but well-defined) cannot — the value is taken. The chosen sentinel collides with a legitimate value of the same type.
  3. Pattern-matching on `{ limit: 0 }` is fragile: a refactor that changes limit to a smaller integer type, or that adds derive(Default) and starts initialising limit to 0 in error paths, silently flips the meaning of every `TooManyChanges { limit: 0 }` site.
  4. `Display` no longer agrees with the Debug derive: a debug-printed TooManyChanges { limit: 0 } looks like a too-many-changes error of zero items, not the documented "cannot calculate" reset.

The idiomatic Rust shape is two variants:

  #[non_exhaustive]
  pub enum BackendChangesError<E> {
      /// Backend has no usable change log for this state window — client
      /// MUST discard cached state and full-resync. RFC 8620 §5.6.
      CannotCalculate,
      /// Window exceeds what the backend can supply in one response;
      /// `limit` is the suggested smaller window the client may retry.
      TooManyChanges { limit: u64 },
      Other(E),
  }

Display then has two intuitive arms, From has two intuitive arms, and the magic-zero discriminator disappears. The variant rename collides with no current consumer because the type is `#[non_exhaustive]` and the From-impl is the only public-API consumer in this crate; downstream backends pattern-match on the construct they care about and were already paying the readability cost.

Relevant Rust-idiom rule: "bool parameters / two-variant enums in disguise" — even though limit:u64 looks like a numeric payload, the documented contract gives one of its values (0) a non-numeric meaning. That is a two-variant enum in disguise.

Fix is mechanically safe: the BackendChangesError type is `#[non_exhaustive]`, so adding a `CannotCalculate` variant is an additive change. The deprecation path is to land both variants, migrate the From impl, document `TooManyChanges { limit: 0 }` as deprecated for the same effect, then drop the magic-zero handling at the next major.
